 awed by the power of the machines and the men who operated them. But after a few years of working every summer and holiday from school in the one industry of any size in his southwestern Wisconsin hometown of Muscoda, Tim Ebert knew it wasn't for him. Still, he stayed with it until he could afford to go to college and study business, which he did at the University of Wisconsin-Whitewater. By then Mark and Pat Ebert, his parents, had taken over Log Cabin Lumber, the sawmill his grandfather, a German immigrant named Henry Ruhstorfer, had started in 1936. The elder Eberts had worked for Henry, and when Henry died in 1972, they bought the business from his widow—with a loan from Henry's son, Ervin, who worked there too. Tim started working there as well after he graduated from college in 1985 and became president of the company in 1992, when his father retired.
